      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The late 90s saw a surge in reptilian horror following the success of <a href=\"/movie/329\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"329\">Jurassic Park</a> (1993). Films like Komodo and <a href=\"/movie/41275\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"41275\">Python</a> (2000) attempted to capitalize on advances in CGI while working with fraction of the budgets. What makes Komodo interesting is its attempt to ground the threat in a real animal species, unlike the purely fictional creatures of <a href=\"/movie/53850\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"53850\">Basilisk: The Serpent King</a> (2006) or the prehistoric monsters of <a href=\"/movie/4283\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"4283\">Primeval</a> (2007). SUBHEAD: The Rise of Natural Horror"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Natural horror films featuring real animals rather than supernatural creatures gained significant traction in the late 90s and early 2000s. <a href=\"/movie/8914\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8914\">Deep Blue Sea</a> (1999) used genetic engineering to make its sharks more threatening, while <a href=\"/movie/9360\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"9360\">Anaconda</a> (1997) relied on the exotic nature of its South American setting. Komodo follows this template but struggles with its effects budget, resulting in more implied threat than visible monster action."
      }
